1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:03,460
Twenty-seven moves. A close game.

2
00:00:03,875 --> 00:00:07,095
Then one pawn grab… and it's mate.

3
00:00:07,490 --> 00:00:09,630
So what should you have played?

4
00:00:09,985 --> 00:00:11,525
This is Chess Review.

5
00:00:11,545 --> 00:00:14,205
Free game review
for Chess.com and Lichess,

6
00:00:14,225 --> 00:00:16,665
with Stockfish running
right in your browser.

7
00:00:17,020 --> 00:00:19,740
Finish a game, and click
Analyze with Chess Review.

8
00:00:19,760 --> 00:00:22,320
Or paste a link, or a PGN.

9
00:00:23,030 --> 00:00:26,110
Every move is classified,
from Brilliant to Blunder,

10
00:00:26,130 --> 00:00:28,470
and the graph shows where the game turned.

11
00:00:29,080 --> 00:00:33,010
Move 28, queen takes d4. A mistake.

12
00:00:33,030 --> 00:00:36,160
The arrow shows what held. Rook to g1.

13
00:00:36,180 --> 00:00:38,580
Try your own idea, and it's rated too.

14
00:00:38,600 --> 00:00:40,900
Pawn to h3? Still mate.

15
00:00:41,180 --> 00:00:43,480
You also get accuracy for both players,

16
00:00:43,500 --> 00:00:45,840
and an estimated performance rating.

17
00:00:45,980 --> 00:00:47,960
Then, practice your mistakes.

18
00:00:47,980 --> 00:00:50,000
Chess Review takes you back to the moment…

19
00:00:50,020 --> 00:00:51,920
and you find the better move.

20
00:00:53,490 --> 00:00:54,790
Choose from ten coaches,

21
00:00:54,810 --> 00:00:56,950
and the board and pieces you like.

22
00:00:57,480 --> 00:01:00,720
No account. No subscription. Open source.

23
00:01:00,830 --> 00:01:03,790
Chess Review.
Free on the Chrome Web Store.
